{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 2 November 2026 ballot what is it polling","content":"**California Proposition 2 (November 3, 2026 ballot) is a legislatively referred constitutional amendment (ACA 20) titled “Increases State’s Rainy Day Fund.”** It would raise the cap on the state’s Budget Stabilization Account (rainy day fund, created by voters in 2014) from 10% to 20% of General Fund tax revenues, require larger deposits in years with very high capital gains tax revenue, extend extra debt payments through 2040 (from 2030) with expanded uses (including certain school payments, other state funds, and federal unemployment loans), and change Gann Limit (state spending cap) rules so rainy day deposits do not count toward the limit when made (they count when withdrawn). The Legislative Analyst’s Office notes this would lead to higher state budget reserves. It passed the Legislature with strong support (Assembly 59-8, Senate 29-2) and has no formal opposition listed in the voter guide. Supporters include California Professional Firefighters, the LA Chamber of Commerce, Gov. Gavin Newsom, and others.[[1]](https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/2)

**Polling (among likely voters):** The most recent public poll is from the Public Policy Institute of California (PPIC) Statewide Survey (Oct. 16–22, 2026): **61% Yes, 38% No, 1% undecided**. An earlier PPIC poll (Sept. 4–10, 2026) showed 60% Yes and 39% No. Support is strongly partisan (around 85% among Democrats, 23% among Republicans, 57% among independents) but holds at 50%+ across most regions, ages, and demographics. Independent forecasts (e.g., On Point Politics) averaged around 61% Yes and projected ~62.6% Yes with a very high chance of passage.[[2]](https://ballotpedia.org/California_Proposition_2,_Budget_Stabilization_Account_Cap_Increase_and_Gann_Limit_Changes_Amendment_(2026))

These figures come from pre-election surveys; unofficial early results on election night showed even stronger support in initial reporting, but full certified results follow the canvass. For the official voter guide, analysis, and arguments, see the California Secretary of State site.","citations":["https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/2","https://ballotpedia.org/California_Proposition_2,_Budget_Stabilization_Account_Cap_Increase_and_Gann_Limit_Changes_Amendment_(2026)"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":""Proposition 2" California results November 2026 yes no","content":"**Proposition 2 (Increases State’s Rainy Day Fund, a legislatively referred constitutional amendment) is on California’s November 3, 2026, general election ballot.** There are no certified or unofficial vote totals yet, as the election has not occurred (or results have not been reported). Check the California Secretary of State’s election results site after polls close for live unofficial counts.[[1]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

It would double the Budget Stabilization Account (rainy day fund) cap from 10% to 20% of General Fund tax revenues, require larger deposits in high-revenue years (especially from capital gains), extend extra debt payments through 2039–40 (adding certain unemployment fund obligations), and adjust how deposits/withdrawals count against the Gann spending limit. A yes vote supports these changes to build larger reserves for downturns; a no vote keeps current rules. It needs a simple majority to pass.[[2]](https://voterguide.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2/title-summary.htm)

Official details, analysis, and the voter guide are available from the California Secretary of State and Legislative Analyst’s Office. Results will be certified later (typically by mid-December). For the latest, visit electionresults.sos.ca.gov.","citations":["https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county","https://voterguide.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2/title-summary.htm"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 2 2026 forecast probability pass On Point Politics Kalshi","content":"**On Point Politics forecasts a 99.4% chance that California Proposition 2 (2026) will pass**, with a projected Yes vote share of 62.6% (needing a simple majority of 50%). This is based on their model using polling (average ~61% Yes), historical comparable measures, and simulations (updated November 2, 2026, one day before the November 3 election). Yes is projected to carry 56 of 58 counties.[[1]](https://onpointpolitics.us/forecast/measures/ca/proposition-2)

**Kalshi’s prediction market implied ~92% probability** of passage as of November 2, 2026 (based on contract prices for the “Save for California’s Future Act”). Earlier snapshots showed it around 82–92% depending on the date. Volume on the specific Prop 2 market was relatively modest compared to higher-profile races.[[2]](https://predictionscout.com/research/state-ballot-measure-prediction-markets-2026/)

Proposition 2 is a legislatively referred constitutional amendment to double the state’s rainy-day fund (Budget Stabilization Account) cap from 10% to 20% of General Fund tax revenues, require larger deposits in high-revenue years, extend extra debt payments, and adjust Gann Limit treatment of reserves. It has bipartisan legislative support and limited organized opposition. Polls (e.g., PPIC) have shown consistent ~60–61% Yes support among likely voters. Other markets like Manifold were around 90% earlier in the cycle. These are pre-election snapshots and can shift with late voting or results.","citations":["https://onpointpolitics.us/forecast/measures/ca/proposition-2","https://predictionscout.com/research/state-ballot-measure-prediction-markets-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}

